D.para('**Mendelian randomization study of remnant cholesterol in relation to non-alcoholic fatty liver disease**',align='left',size=19,after=8)
D.para('Alireza Soleymani T^^a^^, Mahdi Akbarzadeh^^b^^, Danial Habibi^^c,^^*',align='left',size=12,after=8)
abstract=(f"Although observational data suggest a relationship between remnant cholesterol (RC) and non-alcoholic fatty liver disease (NAFLD), there remain questions about causality. RC is the cholesterol carried by triglyceride-rich lipoproteins and their remnants, and the liver is both the source of these particles and the organ that stores their triglyceride. Mendelian randomization (MR) is a method that uses genetic variants as instruments for making causal inferences about an exposure and an outcome. For the exposure variable, we selected 52 independent genome-wide significant variants for RC from a UK Biobank GWAS of 115,082 participants. We then applied GWAS summary statistics for NAFLD from 3 datasets: Ghodsian et al (8,434 cases and 770,180 controls), Fairfield et al (4,761 cases and 373,227 controls) and FinnGen release 5 (894 cases and 217,898 controls). Genetically predicted RC showed no association with NAFLD (Ghodsian: OR = {gi['OR']:.3f}, 95% CI: {gi['lo']:.3f}–{gi['hi']:.3f}, P = {pf(gi['p'])}; Fairfield: OR = {fi['OR']:.3f}, 95% CI: {fi['lo']:.3f}–{fi['hi']:.3f}, P = {pf(fi['p'])}; FinnGen: OR = {ni['OR']:.3f}, 95% CI: {ni['lo']:.3f}–{ni['hi']:.3f}, P = {pf(ni['p'])}). There was substantial heterogeneity [Q~~IVW~~ = {S[G]['Q']:.1f}, P = {pf(S[G]['Q_p'])}; I^^2^^ = {100*het.loc['Ghodsian','I2_IVW']:.1f}%; I^^2^^~~GX~~ = {100*het.loc['Ghodsian','I2_GX']:.2f}%] but no evidence of directional pleiotropy [MR-Egger intercept = {S[G]['egger_int']:.4f}, P = {pf(S[G]['egger_int_p'])}] in the first dataset. This finding was consistently supported across multiple analytical methods, including MR-PRESSO (OR = {pr('Ghodsian')['OR']:.3f}, 95% CI: {pr('Ghodsian')['lo']:.3f}–{pr('Ghodsian')['hi']:.3f}, P = {pf(pr('Ghodsian')['p'])}), and the same instruments were strongly associated with coronary artery disease as a positive control (OR = {pcR.iloc[0].OR:.3f}, 95% CI: {pcR.iloc[0].OR_lo:.3f}–{pcR.iloc[0].OR_hi:.3f}). We found no strong evidence of a causal association between genetically predicted RC and NAFLD under the current study design; however, moderate effects cannot be excluded.")

p=D.para('**Abstract**',align='left',size=11,after=2,keep=True)
